==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|dewes||two}}, from {{der|en|xno|-}}, from {{der|en|fro|deus}}, from {{der|en|la|duo}}. The word was used by Ford to describe a model of car they made in 1932 due to it being a [[two-seater]].

====Pronunciation====
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/djuːs/|/d͡ʒuːs/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/duːs/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-deuce.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|uːs|s=1}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|cards}} A card with two [[pip#Etymology 3|pip]]s, one of four in a standard [[deck]] of [[playing card]]s.
#*: {{quote-song|en|artist=w:Tex Ritter|date=1948-01-01|album=Famous Country Music Makers|track=20|title=Deck of Cards|url=https://genius.com/Tex-ritter-deck-of-cards-lyrics|passage=You see, Sir, when I look at the Ace it reminds me that there is but one God. The '''deuce''' reminds me that the bible is divided into two parts; the Old and New Testaments. And when I see the trey I think of the Father, Son and Holy Ghost.}}
# {{lb|en|dice}} A side of a [[die]] with two spots.
# {{lb|en|dice}} A [[cast]] of dice totalling two.
# The number [[two]].
## {{lb|en|North America|slang}} A [[piece]] of [[excrement]].
## {{lb|en|Canada|slang}} A two-year prison sentence.
# A hand gesture consisting of a raised index and middle fingers, a [[peace sign]].
# {{lb|en|tennis}} A [[tie]]d game where either player can win by scoring two [[consecutive]] points.
# {{lb|en|baseball}} A [[curveball]].
# A {{w|1932 Ford}}.
#* {{quote-song|en|date=1973-01-05|artist=w:Bruce Springsteen|album=Greetings from Asbury Park, N.J.|track=1|title=Blinded by the Light|passage=And she was blinded by the light/Oh, cut loose like a '''deuce''', another runner in the night.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1978|author=Joe Mayall|chapter=Driving Impression: Reproduction '''Deuce''' Hiboy|title=Rod Action|page=26}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=2012|author=Pat Ganahl|title=Lost Hot Rods II: More Remarkable Stories of How They Were Found|page=62|passage=It belonged to “the 1932 guy,” who had four or five '''Deuces''' sitting in his yard.}}
<!--- '''1999''', Geisert, Eric. "The California Spyder", in ''Street Rodder'', p.34 --->
# {{lb|en|in the plural}} 2-[[Venturi effect|barrel]] (twin [[choke]]) [[carburetor]]s {{qualifier|in the phrase ''3 deuces'': an arrangement on a common [[intake manifold]]}}.
# {{lb|en|restaurants|slang}} A [[table]] seating [[two]] [[diner]]s.

